export const editionLogarithms=m`<p>This is a new modern-English rendering from the public-domain French of Euler’s <i>De la controverse entre Mrs. Leibnitz &amp; Bernoulli sur les logarithmes des nombres négatifs et imaginaires</i>, Eneström E168, printed in 1751 in the Berlin academy’s volume for 1749, pp. 139–179. The Euler Archive records 1747 as the written date; the URL uses the actual publication year. This reading covers the whole paper: the opening, both positions, all seven supporting arguments, all nine objections, the replies, the theorem, four problems, all ten numbered examples, the tables of logarithm values, and the closing calculation.</p><p>The basis is the 41-page original French scan linked above. We read its complete prose with local OCR as an aid and inspected the critical formulas directly. The independent English translation by Stacy G. Langton, University of San Diego, dated 1999 on its first page, is linked for comparison, not reproduced. Its translator’s notes are not part of Euler’s text and are not imported as authorial prose. This is a modern rendering rather than a diplomatic transcription: repeated verbal formulas and enumerations are consolidated where the complete mathematical content remains explicit. In the ten examples, real and imaginary coordinates give the source’s separate sine and cosine values, and both sign progressions are retained.</p><p>We replace the source’s logarithm letter by \(\log\), \(\sqrt{-1}\) by \(i\), consecutive products by factorials, and \(aa,bb\) by \(a^2,b^2\). Even and odd index letters keep their declared ranges. Our passage labels and anchors subdivide unnumbered prose editorially; they are not invented section numbers. The original separately refers to a plate for the logarithmic curve; the margin drawing is an editorial reconstruction, not a reproduction of that plate. Local printing repairs are identified beside the passages: \(x=0\) on p. 149 and \(q=1+i\varphi/n\) on p. 166. The negative-logarithm formulas follow the original’s \(\sqrt{-1}\), where the English comparison file sometimes prints \(\sqrt{-a}\). Mathematical defects such as unqualified power laws, divergent substitutions, or unconstrained infinite-root limits remain visible and receive separate commentary.</p><p>Our notation \(\mathcal L(z)\) for all logarithms, the explicit exclusion of zero, and the distinction between scaling a family and independently adding its values are editorial. Four written reconstructions provide the complete inverse-exponential classification, the fixed-index root limit, the relevant operations on families and roots, and the differential-equation argument; a fifth supplies finite series remainders. They stay within the finite real and complex expressions used here. These are written proofs, without proof-assistant verification. <a href="../euler-logarithm-source.json">Source evidence and checksums →</a></p>`;
